+\chapter{Residue traces and three-current chains}
+\label{chap:collision-chains}
+
+The double pole contributes a derivative of the test function on a pair
+diagonal.  That derivative must enter the source differential of a current
+comparison.  For three elementary currents, a pair contraction leaves one
+current.  The three pair diagonals therefore supply a finite complex and an
+explicit map to compact boundary observables.  The regular vertex product
+has a separate obstruction: its associator survives in boundary cohomology.
+
+\section{The differential on test chains}
+
+Fix $0<r<\rho<R$, put $A=D_r$, $U=D_R$, and let $\chi$ be a smooth
+radial function supported compactly in $U$, equal to one on $D_\rho$.
+Use the complex orientation, $dA=dx\,dy$, and
+$\int^{\mathrm{res}}=(2\pi i)^{-1}\int$.
+Work over $\C[\hbar]$, with fixed $\kappa\in\C$ and
+$\lambda=-\hbar\kappa$ of degree zero.
+The target is the actual smooth boundary complex
+\[
+ B=\mathcal B_\kappa(U),\qquad
+ D=\bar\partial+\hbar\Delta_{\mu_\kappa},\qquad
+ \mu_\kappa(f,v)=-\kappa\int_U^{\mathrm{res}}f\,\partial v.
+\]
+A function generator has degree $-1$ and a form generator has degree zero.
+Each symmetric power uses compact smooth kernels and graded coinvariants.
+The direct sum in field number remains algebraic.
+Set
+\[
+ e_{a,n}=\partial_a^n\left(-\frac{\bar\partial\chi}{z-a}\right),
+ \qquad e_a=e_{a,0},\qquad S=\operatorname{supp}\bar\partial\chi.
+\]
+These forms have support in $S\Subset U$ and depend holomorphically on
+$a\in A$ in every smooth seminorm.
+For three elementary insertions, fix the distribution
+\[
+ W=e_{z_1}e_{z_2}e_{z_3}
+   +\lambda\sum_{i<j}E_2(z_i-z_j)e_{z_k},
+ \qquad \{k\}=\{1,2,3\}\setminus\{i,j\}.
+\]
+Here $E_2=-\partial_z(1/z)$ acts on test densities, as in
+Theorem~\ref{thm:cd-three-extension}.  The products of form generators
+are symmetric smooth kernels in the field variables.  The insertion
+variables remain distinct from those field variables.
+
+For an open $Y\subset\C^d$ with the displayed affine coordinates, define
+the complex of compact Dolbeault test chains by
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-test-differential}
+ K_Y^{-j}=C_c^\infty(Y,\operatorname{Dens}_{\C}
+             \otimes\Lambda^j T^{0,1}Y)[\hbar],\qquad
+ \delta_Y=-\sum_{a=1}^d\partial_{\bar z_a}\iota_{d\bar z_a}.
+\end{equation}
+The index satisfies $0\le j\le d$.  Densities are complexified real
+densities, with coefficient derivatives taken in the coordinate area
+density.  Exterior contraction acts on the multivector factor.
+The differential has degree one, and $\delta_Y^2=0$ because coefficient
+derivatives commute while exterior contractions anticommute.
+Use the test-function direct-limit topology on each degree.
+The distributional pairing satisfies
+\[
+ \langle\bar\partial T,\alpha\rangle
+       =\langle T,\delta_Y\alpha\rangle
+\]
+for a distribution-valued antiholomorphic form $T$ and a test chain of
+the matching exterior degree.  This equality fixes every transpose sign.
+
+Put $X=A^3$.  For $i<j$, let $Z_{ij}=\{z_i=z_j\}\cap X$ and fix
+coordinates
+\[
+ x=z_i-z_j,\qquad s=z_j,\qquad w=z_k.
+\]
+The real Jacobian has absolute determinant one.  On $Z_{ij}$ use
+coordinates $(s,w)$ and density $dA_s dA_w$.
+In these coordinates define a degree-one residue trace
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-residue-trace}
+ r_{ij}:K_X\longrightarrow K_{Z_{ij}},\qquad
+ r_{ij}\alpha=
+ \left.\pi\partial_x\iota_{d\bar x}\alpha\right|_{x=0}.
+\end{equation}
+The formula removes the normal density factor $dA_x$ after differentiation
+and restriction.  Exterior factors remaining after contraction are
+tangent to $Z_{ij}$.  In particular $r_{ij}$ vanishes on degree zero.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Residue trace and Dolbeault differential]
+\label{lem:cc-residue-trace}
+The traces are continuous on fixed compact supports, preserve compactness,
+and satisfy
+\[
+ \delta_{Z_{ij}}r_{ij}+r_{ij}\delta_X=0.
+\]
+For $\alpha\in K_X^{-1}$,
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-contact-pairing}
+ \langle W,\delta_X\alpha\rangle
+   =\lambda\sum_{i<j}\langle e_w,r_{ij}\alpha\rangle.
+\end{equation}
+The right side integrates both variables of $Z_{ij}$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Differentiate a compact test coefficient once in $x$ and restrict to
+$x=0$.  The result has support in the intersection of its compact support
+with $Z_{ij}$, and its seminorms require one additional derivative.
+Write $\iota_x=\iota_{d\bar x}$.  The normal term of
+$r_{ij}\delta_X$ vanishes by $\iota_x^2=0$.
+For a tangential coordinate $v=s,w$, one has
+$\iota_x\iota_{d\bar v}=-\iota_{d\bar v}\iota_x$.
+Tangential derivatives commute with $\partial_x$ and restriction.
+This proves the anticommutation identity.
+
+The normalized residue is
+$\bar\partial E_2(x)=-\pi\partial_x\delta_0(x)d\bar x$.
+Its action on the normal test coefficient is
+$\pi\partial_x\alpha_x(0,s,w)$.
+The surviving form $e_w$ is independent of $x$ and holomorphic in $w$.
+Consequently differentiation of $W$ gives precisely the right side of
+\eqref{eq:cc-contact-pairing}.  The uncontracted term is holomorphic in
+all three insertion variables and contributes zero.
+\end{proof}
+
+\section{A comparison including the pair diagonals}
+
+On the direct sum of test-chain complexes define
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-source}
+ \begin{split}
+ K_{\mathrm{res}}&=K_X\oplus\bigoplus_{i<j}K_{Z_{ij}},\\
+ d_{\mathrm{res}}(\alpha,(\beta_{ij}))
+  &=\left(\delta_X\alpha,
+       (\delta_{Z_{ij}}\beta_{ij}-\lambda r_{ij}\alpha)_{i<j}\right).
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+Each diagonal summand records a contracted pair and its surviving current.
+It has the same test-chain grading as its geometric dimension prescribes.
+The quotient by the diagonal summands is the original complex $K_X$.
+The inclusion of $K_X$ as a graded summand is not a chain map when
+$\lambda r_{ij}$ is nonzero.
+
+Define $F:K_{\mathrm{res}}\to B$ to be zero in negative degrees and
+to have degree-zero component
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-map}
+ F(\alpha,(\beta_{ij}))
+    =\langle W,\alpha\rangle+
+               \sum_{i<j}\langle e_w,\beta_{ij}\rangle.
+\end{equation}
+All test coefficients in this equation are smooth compact densities.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Three-current residue comparison]
+\label{thm:cc-residue-comparison}
+The differential in \eqref{eq:cc-source} has square zero, and
+$DF=F d_{\mathrm{res}}$.
+The image consists of compact smooth observable kernels supported in
+$S$ and $S^3$, together with their finite linear sums.
+The maps are continuous on fixed compact test supports.
+Their test supports may meet every pair diagonal and the total diagonal.
+
+Let $A_I=\C[t,dt]$ with $d_It=dt$, $(dt)^2=0$, and the increasing
+orientation on $[0,1]$.  With the form factor first, the map
+\[
+ \mathfrak F=1\otimes F:
+ (A_I\otimes K_{\mathrm{res}},d_I+d_{\mathrm{res}})
+       \longrightarrow (A_I\otimes B,d_I+D)
+\]
+is a chain map.  In each total differential the second summand acts with
+the sign $(-1)^{|\gamma|}$ on $\gamma\otimes a$.
+Endpoint evaluation gives the chain map $F$ on constant interval forms.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+The diagonal component of $d_{\mathrm{res}}^2$ is
+$-\lambda(\delta_Zr+r\delta_X)\alpha=0$ by
+Lemma~\ref{lem:cc-residue-trace}.  The other terms vanish by
+$\delta_X^2=\delta_Z^2=0$.
+Thus the differential includes its entire degree-two compatibility.
+No additional contraction is possible after pairing two of three
+elementary currents: the contracted pair is scalar and only one field
+remains.  This is why this complex needs no second contraction term.
+
+Every output in \eqref{eq:cc-map} has only form generators.  Both its
+internal Dolbeault differential and its quantum contraction vanish.
+Thus $DF=0$.
+In degree $-1$, the contact identity gives
+\[
+ Fd_{\mathrm{res}}(\alpha,\beta)
+ =\lambda\sum_{i<j}\langle e_w,r_{ij}\alpha\rangle
+  +\sum_{i<j}\langle e_w,\delta_Z\beta_{ij}\rangle
+  -\lambda\sum_{i<j}\langle e_w,r_{ij}\alpha\rangle=0.
+\]
+The middle sum vanishes by holomorphic dependence and integration by
+parts.  In lower degrees, the target component of $F$ is zero on both
+sides.  In degree zero, the source differential vanishes.
+This proves the chain equation in every degree.
+
+The estimate $|z-a|\ge\rho-r$ on the field annulus gives uniform smooth
+bounds for the unpaired forms.  Each coefficient distribution has finite
+order on a compact insertion support.
+Pairing therefore produces a smooth field kernel with support in the
+same annulus product, as in Proposition~\ref{prop:cd-smeared}.
+The trace terms have the analogous estimate with one normal derivative.
+No parameter support excludes the total diagonal.
+Finally tensoring two cochain maps, with the stated sign, proves the
+interval equation.  Endpoint evaluation kills $dt$ and commutes with
+the total differential.
+\end{proof}
+
+The same construction retains every holomorphic jet of each elementary
+current.  For fixed $n_1,n_2,n_3\ge0$, replace $W$ by
+\[
+ W_{\mathbf n}=
+ \prod_{i=1}^3e_{z_i,n_i}
+ +\lambda\sum_{i<j}(-1)^{n_i}(n_i+n_j+1)!
+        E_{n_i+n_j+2}(z_i-z_j)e_{z_k,n_k}.
+\]
+Replace the trace by
+\[
+ r_{ij}^{\mathbf n}\alpha
+  =\left.\pi(-1)^{n_i}\partial_x^{n_i+n_j+1}
+                     \iota_{d\bar x}\alpha\right|_{x=0}.
+\]
+The action of
+$\bar\partial E_m=\pi(-1)^{m-1}\partial_x^{m-1}\delta_0\,d\bar x/(m-1)!$
+cancels both factorials and transpose signs, giving this trace.
+It anticommutes with $\delta$ by the same exterior calculation.
+Thus the theorem holds for the direct sum over all finite jet choices.
+For divided jets, use $e_{z_i,n_i}/n_i!$ for each uncontracted input.
+Divide $r_{ij}^{\mathbf n}$ by $n_i!n_j!$, and use
+$e_{w,n_k}/n_k!$ in the diagonal output.
+The complete degree-zero Wick distribution is then
+$W_{\mathbf n}/(n_1!n_2!n_3!)$.
+
+This source specifies elementary three-current contractions and their
+jets.  For three composite insertions, two contractions can survive, and
+three quadratic insertions contain the triangle coefficient
+$8\lambda^3T_\triangle$.
+The derivative of its distributional extension is already defined by
+\eqref{eq:cd-triangle-contact}.  A source for those composite collision
+chains also requires maps between its contraction strata.  The complex
+\eqref{eq:cc-source} does not supply those additional maps.
+
+\section{The maximal test subcomplex for a fixed Wick smearing}
+
+The same contact condition has an exact formulation for all finite
+polynomial insertions, without choosing products of residue currents.
+Let $V=\C[q_1,q_2,\ldots][\hbar]$ be the normal polynomial state space,
+and put $M=V\otimes_{\C[\hbar]}V\otimes_{\C[\hbar]}V$.
+All states have degree zero, and every state has finite polynomial degree.
+Let $W_M$ be the complete three-insertion Wick distribution with these
+inputs, applying $\mathcal E_3$ to each rational coefficient and using
+the same annular forms for the unpaired fields.
+Specifically, the divided state $q_{n+1}$ at $a$ uses $e_{a,n}/n!$.
+This is precisely the map of Proposition~\ref{prop:cd-smeared}.
+Put $K=K_X\otimes_{\C[\hbar]}M$, and define
+\[
+ \Phi:K^0\to B^0,\quad \Phi(\alpha)=\langle W_M,\alpha\rangle,
+ \qquad C:K^{-1}\to B^0,\quad C(\alpha)=\Phi(\delta\alpha).
+\]
+Equivalently $C$ pairs $\bar\partial W_M$ with $\alpha$.
+Since $\delta^2=0$, one has $C\delta=0$ on $K^{-2}$.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Detection of annular boundaries]
+\label{lem:cc-annular-detection}
+Suppose $b\in B^0$ is a finite sum of outputs of $\Phi$ or $C$.
+If $b=Dq$ for some $q\in B^{-1}$, then $b=0$ as a smooth observable.
+The same conclusion holds if $1\otimes b$ is exact in $A_I\otimes B$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Let $u_m=-\bar\partial\chi/z^{m+1}$ for $m\ge0$.
+On the annulus,
+\[
+ e_a=\sum_{m\ge0}a^m u_m.
+\]
+Choose $r_0$ strictly above the radii of a compact parameter support,
+with $0<r_0<r$, and choose $\sigma$ between $r$ and $\rho$.
+For a multi-index $\mathbf m$, distributional finite order and the
+annular denominator give bounds
+\[
+ |c_{\mathbf m}|\le C(1+|\mathbf m|)^N r_0^{|\mathbf m|},\qquad
+ \|u_{m_1}\cdots u_{m_s}\|_{C^k}
+       \le C_k(1+|\mathbf m|)^{N_k}\sigma^{-|\mathbf m|}.
+\]
+Jets add a fixed power of $|\mathbf m|$.
+The ratio $r_0/\sigma<1$ proves convergence after every smooth derivative.
+In field number $s$, each output therefore has a convergent symmetric
+multiple series in $u_{m_1}\cdots u_{m_s}$.
+
+The Cauchy pairing of two $u_m$ vanishes by rotation invariance.
+Continuity then implies $N_Ub=b$, where $dN_U=N_UD$ is
+Proposition~\ref{prop:bf-cauchy-conjugation}.
+For $L\ge0$, define the classical moment map to the polynomial algebra
+$\C[y_0,\ldots,y_L][\hbar]$ by
+\[
+ R_L(v)=\sum_{m=0}^L\left(\int_U^{\mathrm{res}}v\wedge z^m dz\right)y_m,
+ \qquad R_L(f)=0.
+\]
+On each smooth symmetric kernel, integrate the indicated moment in every
+field variable.  Holomorphicity and compact integration by parts give
+$R_Ld=0$.  Hence $L_L=R_LN_U$ satisfies $L_LD=0$.
+The residue moments obey $R_L(u_m)=y_m$ for $m\le L$ and vanish otherwise.
+Thus the family $R_L$ detects every coefficient of the convergent symmetric
+series.  If $b=Dq$, then $R_Lb=L_LDq=0$ for every $L$.
+Every coefficient is zero, so the smooth kernel is zero in each field
+number.  Only finitely many field numbers occur.
+For the interval assertion, evaluate an exact expression at either
+endpoint, obtaining $b=D(\operatorname{ev}_a q)$, and apply the first part.
+\end{proof}
+
+Define a subcomplex $K_{\mathrm{ker}}\subseteq K$ by
+\[
+ K_{\mathrm{ker}}^0=K^0,\qquad
+ K_{\mathrm{ker}}^{-1}=\ker C,\qquad
+ K_{\mathrm{ker}}^{-j}=K^{-j}\quad(j=2,3).
+\]
+The equation $C\delta=0$ proves that the displayed spaces form a
+subcomplex.  Equip the kernel with the induced test-chain topology.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Maximal domain with unchanged test differential]
+\label{thm:cc-maximal-domain}
+The map $\Phi$, extended by zero in negative degrees, is a continuous
+chain map $K_{\mathrm{ker}}\to B$.
+If a subcomplex $L\subseteq K$ contains $K^0$ and a chain map
+$G:L\to A_I\otimes B$ satisfies $G|_{K^0}=1\otimes\Phi$, then
+$L\subseteq K_{\mathrm{ker}}$.
+The same conclusion holds if this degree-zero equality is changed by an
+exact observable for each input.
+Thus $K_{\mathrm{ker}}$ is the largest subcomplex with this degree-zero
+smearing and the unchanged test differential, even when negative-degree
+comparison maps are allowed.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Every $\Phi$ output is a $D$-cycle.  On degree $-1$, the chain equation
+is precisely $\Phi\delta=C=0$.  Lower degrees contribute zero, proving
+the first assertion.  Continuity follows from the fixed-support finite
+order estimates for $\mathcal E_3$ and the annular kernels.
+For $\alpha\in L^{-1}$, the chain equation for $G$ implies that
+$1\otimes C\alpha$ is exact.  The same implication holds if the
+degree-zero map is changed by an exact observable.
+Lemma~\ref{lem:cc-annular-detection} gives $C\alpha=0$.
+Thus $L^{-1}\subseteq\ker C$.  All other degree inclusions follow from
+the definition of $K_{\mathrm{ker}}$.
+\end{proof}
+
+The restriction is strict at nonzero level.  Choose a small coordinate
+box around $x=0$, $s=s_0$, $w=w_0$, with $s_0\ne w_0$.
+Require its closure to lie in $X$ and avoid both other pair diagonals.
+Choose compact smooth functions $\theta(x)$ and $\psi(s,w)$ there,
+with $\theta=1$ near zero and $\int\psi\,dA_s dA_w=1$.
+For three elementary currents take
+\[
+ \alpha=x\theta(x)\psi(s,w)\,dA_xdA_sdA_w
+                     \otimes\partial_{\bar x}.
+\]
+The only residue is $r_{12}\alpha=\pi\psi\,dA_s dA_w$.
+Consequently $C\alpha=\pi\lambda\int\psi(s,w)e_w\,dA_sdA_w$.
+Its first constant holomorphic moment is $\pi\lambda\ne0$ when
+$\kappa\ne0$.  Thus neither a negative-degree boundary observable nor
+an interval primitive can extend the smearing to this test chain.
+Tests supported off the pair diagonals have zero contact and belong to
+$K_{\mathrm{ker}}$ in every degree.
+
+\section{The regular-product associator}
+
+The preceding source differential carries a contact to a surviving
+current.  An ordinary ternary homotopy with three closed state inputs
+has a different equation.  Let
+$\iota:V\to B$ send $q_{m+1}$ to $u_m$.
+The regular vertex product is the constant Laurent coefficient of
+$Y(a,z)b$.  Its elementary calculation gives
+\[
+ (q_1{}_{(-1)}q_1)_{(-1)}q_1
+     -q_1{}_{(-1)}(q_1{}_{(-1)}q_1)=2\lambda q_3.
+\]
+Indeed $J_-(z)=q_1+q_2z+q_3z^2+\cdots$, and
+$:J(z)^2:q_1=J_-(z)^2q_1+2\lambda z^{-2}J_-(z)$.
+The constant coefficient is $q_1^3+2\lambda q_3$.
+The other parenthesization gives $q_1^3$.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Nonexistence of a ternary boundary primitive]
+\label{prop:cc-ternary-obstruction}
+If $\kappa\ne0$, there is no $h\in(A_I\otimes B)^{-1}$ satisfying
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:cc-ternary-obstruction}
+ (d_I+D)h=1\otimes2\lambda\iota(q_3).
+\end{equation}
+In particular, an uncurved $A_\infty$ algebra on $C_I\otimes V$ with
+unary operation $d_I\otimes1$ cannot have the regular vertex product
+as its binary operation on constant interval cochains at nonzero level.
+The same obstruction excludes an $A_\infty$ structure on a boundary
+resolution whose induced binary product on the injected states is this
+regular product.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Evaluate \eqref{eq:cc-ternary-obstruction} at $t=0$ and apply
+$L_2=R_2N_U$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:cc-annular-detection}, where the subscript
+here denotes the moment bound.  The left side becomes zero.
+The right side becomes $2\lambda y_2$, which is nonzero over
+$\C[\hbar]$ when $\kappa\ne0$.
+Equivalently the $z^2$ moment of $\iota(q_3)=u_2$ equals one.
+
+For three closed degree-zero inputs, the arity-three $A_\infty$ identity
+states that the binary associator is the differential of a degree-minus-one
+ternary value.  The unary input terms vanish.
+Take the three constant interval inputs $(e_0+e_1)q_1$.
+Their regular-product associator is
+$(e_0+e_1)2\lambda q_3$.  It is nonzero in $H^0(C_I\otimes V)=V$.
+Thus the identity cannot hold.  On any boundary resolution with the
+specified injected cohomology classes, the same identity would make its
+cohomological product associative, contradicting the detected associator.
+This is the arity-three consequence of the suspended bar equations in
+Keller~\cite[\S3.6, pp.~11--12]{KellerAInfinity}.
+\end{proof}
+
+For fixed disjoint disk unions, the ordered Taylor maps of
+Theorem~\ref{thm:ordered-current-taylor} retain the target
+$A_I\otimes\mathcal B_\kappa(U_S)$ and the complete Wick product.
+Their nonzero higher coefficients do not supply a primitive for
+\eqref{eq:cc-ternary-obstruction}.  Endpoint evaluation kills their
+coefficients of arity greater than one.
+The residue comparison instead changes the source differential by its
+explicit diagonal terms.  Extending it to composite collision strata and
+relating those strata to iterated regular Laurent coefficients remains a
+separate construction.  The nonzero class $2\lambda q_3$ must be retained
+in that comparison.
